            <ArticleTitle>Broken spinal needle: case report and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>321-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2014.01.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0952-8180(14)00057-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The occurrence of broken spinal and epidural needles has been reported. However, most case reports have focused primarily on prevention rather than on management. A broken spinal needle fragment was left in a patient before it was removed one month later due to back pain.</AbstractText>
